and named here, as its licence asks.

Adapted content
---------------
- claude-coach, by Felix Rieseberg (MIT), https://github.com/felixrieseberg/claude-coach
  The critical swim speed test protocol and its worked example, the swimming zones expressed as
  offsets from critical swim speed, the field-test protocols for running and cycling, and the way
  the knowledge base is split by topic with a "when to read" table.

- Enduragent, formerly cycling-coach, by yerzhansa (MIT), https://github.com/yerzhansa/cycling-coach
  The review structure (did it go well, one thing to fix, what it means next), the filter rule
  (a metric appears only if it answers one of those questions), the evidence-limit rule, the rule
  that every recommendation names its basis, and the choice of plain names over trademarked
  abbreviations.

- Section 11, by CrankAddict (MIT), https://github.com/CrankAddict/section-11, protocol v11.75
  Rules adapted into knowledge/: sleep judged on duration rather than on the device's score,
  calendar illness and injury markers as escalation-only health context, negative form as the
  mechanism of adaptation rather than an alarm, and several of the references it cites.

Read, not copied
----------------
- GoldenCheetah (GPL v2), https://github.com/GoldenCheetah/GoldenCheetah
  Consulted as living documentation of the models (critical power, W′ balance, impulse-response
  load). No GoldenCheetah code is included; the formulas are implemented from the literature.

Science
-------
The models are named where they are implemented: the weighted average power and load framework,
Skiba's W′ balance in its differential form, the Banister impulse-response model as Intervals.icu
implements it, Friel's seven-zone system, Daniels' threshold-relative paces, and critical swim speed.
Full references live in the knowledge base.
